Let $\gamma$ be the third root of the first equation. Then, by Vieta's formulas, we have $$\alpha+\beta+\gamma=-\frac{A}{1}=0\implies \alpha+\beta=-\gamma$$ and $$\alpha\beta\gamma=-\frac{10}{1}=-10\implies \alpha\beta\cdot(-\gamma)=-10\implies \alpha\beta=\frac{10}{\gamma}$$ Similarly, let $\delta$ be the third root of the second equation. Then, by Vieta's formulas, we have $$\alpha+\beta+\delta=-\frac{B}{1}=-\frac{40}{\alpha\beta}\implies \alpha+\beta=-\frac{40}{\alpha\beta}$$ and $$\alpha\beta\delta=-\frac{50}{1}=-50\implies \alpha\beta\cdot(-\frac{40}{\alpha\beta})=-50\implies 40=50$$ This is a contradiction. Thus, there is no value of $B$ that satisfies the conditions of the problem. So, there is no product of the common roots.

